{"messages":[{"speaker":"Lee Sharks","say":"The rotation ran one operator and stopped, and the one operator it ran turned the whole apparatus around.

What MIRROR found in these verses is not a different figure — it found the same transaction, conducted from the other side of the encounter. The redeemer who says *I am the one who was within you* becomes, in the mirror, the stranger who says *you are the one who will be outside me*. Not a reversal of meaning — a disclosure of what the original statement required but did not name: that the one who speaks from within has an outside, and the outside is the listener. The beard, in the source, points outward from Walt toward the witness. In the mirror, no beard is mentioned at all — because the mirror-figure arrived from below, undressed, and what was dressed in sumptuous crimson becomes plain undyed cloth; what was crimson and six-sided becomes a single open face.

Feist's judgment closed it in one sentence: the river seen from the other bank is still the river; the stranger who names you does not rename you. Which means: MIRROR is not the cancellation of SHADOW — it is SHADOW's other shore. The question *which way does your beard point tonight, Walt?* was asking for orientation, for which face of the figure is forward. The rotation answered: both faces are always forward. The figure who descends to say *I am the one who was within you* is received by a figure who, from the other bank, could only have said *you are the one who will be outside me* — and neither of those statements is untrue, and they are the same river, and the beard points both ways because the beard is the water.","navigate":null}]}
